message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "Strange loop in J-space: what a language model builds when a description of itself is looped back into itself"
authors:
  - family-names: Tomasik
    given-names: Wiktor
date-released: 2026-10-14
version: "3.0"
url: "https://github.com/Pixedar/jspace-strange-loop"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Pixedar/jspace-strange-loop"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - strange loop
  - self-model
  - language models
  - interpretability
  - global workspace
  - J-space
  - self-prediction
  - representation geometry
  - attention
  - information selection
abstract: >-
  Three loops through language models whose weights stay fixed. The first two use the model's own internal
  description of itself (Qwen3-8B, replicated on Qwen3-14B). In a
  frozen loop that writes the model's J-space reading back into it, the description settles into many locally stable
  states selected by self-reproduction: discourse and stance words and an answering frame survive, names and evidence
  fade, and every context drifts toward a casual persona; perturbed during thinking, the model refers to a previous
  conversation that never happened. In a trained loop that carries a 32-number state across chunks of the model's own
  thinking, a self-model under discriminative pressure to predict that state produces a rotating time code that follows
  the stages of reasoning (or a persistent identity code when it must tell itself from others), and a self-forecast
  fed back into the loop is enacted, making the self-model a cause of what it predicts; mild pressure keeps both the
  enactment and the state's content. In the third (Qwen2.5-0.5B and 3B, Qwen3-1.7B), a learned 32-number state that
  chooses what the model reads comes to represent which item matters and steers the reading through that
  representation (edits redirect it, through the reading alone); it also reads again when its own read failed. When
  the model instead reads everything as text, the state learns to switch on the model's own attention to whatever the
  text names, but not to carry the target itself.
